<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cimanuk River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cimanuk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cimanuk is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, flowing north from the Garut highlands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a> near Indramayu. In the early sixteenth century the Portuguese writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a> described it as the eastern boundary of the Sunda Kingdom, and its mouth was one of the kingdom's ports, trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cimanuk-river.jpg" alt="Cimanuk River" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aris riyanto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22%2B%2B%22_Ci_Manuk_river_%EA%A6%A7%EA%A6%BC%EA%A6%94%EA%A6%AE%EA%A6%A4%EA%A7%80_%EA%A6%95%EA%A6%B6%EA%A6%A9%EA%A6%A4%EA%A6%B8%EA%A6%8F%EA%A7%80_Kali_Cimanuk_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
